asure of the uh of  a marine environment in the ocean  index  the reef was discovered by a team of  unesco scientists using state-of-the-art  diving equipment that allowed them to  stay deeper for longer periods  you  after 200 hours of diving they measured  the reef at three kilometers long and  stretching down to 70 meters below sea  level  that makes it one of the world's largest  at this depth and raises the prospect of  more reefs waiting to be found  more dives are planned in the coming  months to study the reef and the marine  life that has made it their home  but its discovery shows how little we  still know about our underwater world  katie barnfield sky news  do stay tuned coming up here on the  daily climate show i'll be joined by  member of the committee on climate  change dame julia king and leading  expert in carbon footprinting and  director at small world consulting mike  berners-lee to discuss whether clean air  zones can be made to work  hello and welcome back to the daily  climate show on sky news now we're  getting straight to discussing the  climate issues of the day now with  member of the committee on climate  change dame julia king and leading  expert in carbon footprinting and  director at small world consulting mike  berners-lee say good evening to both of  you very nice to see both  so manchester's council has asked the  government to press pause on plans for  an extended clean air zone the science  is clear and i think it's fair to say  that everyone agrees for the need for  clean air but there's much debate over  how it should be done and who ends up  paying so julia manchester council wants  a review of their clean air zone plans  but is this kind of scheme the right  approach do you think for tackling  pollution  i think it's a very important approach  for tackling pollution  of course as we move towards net zero in  2050  pollution  from vehicles will reduce as we  decarbonize those vehicles but of course  that's not fast enough um for people  like the young man with asthma that we  saw whose health is being affected today  particularly in those  high congestion regions um in a big city  like manchester  but mike it does leave people with that  choice of either upgrading their  vehicles which they say will be really  expensive or paying more to come in and  out of manchester and when we've seen  inflation up to 30-year highs businesses  are squeezed so you can see why this is  going to be tough for people  yeah i mean on the one hand it's  clear-cut that diesel cars in  uh in city centers are you know terrible  not just for climate but also for the  you know the serious impact that they  have on the life expectancy of everyone  uh in that city but on the other hand  yeah we need to implement policies where  as much as possible nobody gets caught  out by them  in principle at least a climate levy  raises a fund and if you're clever with  the money that you have from that fund  you ought to be able to use it to make  sure as far as possible that some that  that fewer people are caught out so it's  what you do with the money that you  raise from a climate levy that uh  that makes it um viable for people  do you think that's that's right julia i  mean what's what's the the best way of  making sure that the air is clean but  without putting that really tough  financial squeeze on people  well i think i think um tim makes a  really good point there that that if you  are charging people to come into the  city centre because they have a heavily  polluting vehicle um it seems a very  good idea to use that tax effectively  for a fund to help small businesses for  example to change to electric vehicles  so mike do you want to expand on that at  all you say a clever use of the funds  but but how can you use them more  cleverly  so for example we heard earlier on your  program somebody talking about how you  know they can't afford to you know spend  the extra money for an electric vehicle  instead of buying a new diesel car for  example well you know maybe you could  use some of that fun to support that  kind of greener purchase of the new  vehicle so that you know there are ways  you know of you can't always get it  perfect but there are ways of making  sure that by and large nobody you know  nobody gets caught out by this in a big  way and we've all got to be remembering  you know that the kid you had on the  program earlier you know those diesel  cars in city centers  really do  uh you know take away significantly from  the life expectancy of the people in the  area we estimated about 12 minutes of  life expectancy taken away from the  population of a city for every mile a  diesel car drives in a traffic jam  through that city so that's a huge  impact on life  and julia i mean i think anybody anyone  driving a vehicle would have real  sympathy with anyone that's suffering  from pollution but there are so many  other  conflicting interests aren't there and  people need to earn a living they need  to put food on the table for their own  children  um yes but i think i think  what uh tim was suggesting is a is a  scheme that makes sure we can help  people to make that transition and we  also need to remember of the huge  benefit to our health system the huge  reduction in cost to the health system  if we have less asthma and less people  suffering from the kind of pollutants  that vehicles um and indeed our gas  central heating  and other things emit in our cities  okay let's move on to our next topic  shall we and just imagine not feeling  guilty about booking a long-haul flight  or at least not having to worry about  the emissions that it'll create that is  in theory at least what could happen in  the future if hydrogen can be produced  cleanly and at scale but there are big  doubts about the technology involved in  getting that hydrogen  cleanly enough now mike i know that  there are lots of different colors of  hydrogen depending on how you produce it  blue hydrogen is one of those different  methods and that involves using fossil  fuels to produce it but then capturing  the co2 emissions from that process do  you think that is a viable option for  the future i noticed that the german  government is setting a lot of store by  hydrogen in the future but it doesn't  think blue hydrogen should be part of  that vision what do you think  well we need to be clear that hydrogen  as a whole has a huge part in the clean  energy transition so  for storage of energy for we need to  work out how to how to generate it we  need to work out how to store it uh we  need to work out how to transport it and  then we need to sort out all the uh all  the ways of using it for everything from  airplanes potentially to ships to use  for steel making so there are many  different hydrogen technologies we need  to develop  and in a way you know they all come  together at once so we need to have a  sufficient supply of hydrogen in order  to be able to roll it out to all those  uses um that we need it for and if  there's a crunch point on  generation of hydrogen then i can  imagine as an interim step that blue  hydrogen  might in theory uh be you know a way of  plugging that gap but certainly for the  long term green hydrogen is a far more  elegant solution  and the technologies for that are coming  along nicely the electrolysis to create  uh green hydrogen you know will  is coming together which will allow us  to generate it at scale in the sunniest  places in the world you know and the  windiest places in the world so long  term it's definitely green hydrogen in  theory at least there's a potential  argument for blue hydrogen although at  the moment it looks very problematic  using it as a in any way a lower carbon  form or you know it's it's pretty well  as high carbon as fossil fuel at the  moment for the reasons that you've  you've outlined in your program already  so far  and i'm going to cut in sorry mike to  cut you off just gonna get julia's take  on this as well if that's okay because  what do you think then julia should we  just be putting all our concentration on  green hydrogen which uses renewable  energy only to produce it i mean  obviously it's the ideal solution but  but it's really expensive and  inefficient at the moment isn't it what  do you think we we have to be practical  and pragmatic about this um in order to  get to net zero in the uk  we have to um increase the size of our  electricity system by probably about two  and a half to three times and that's  assuming that about half our hydrogen is  green hydrogen and half is blue hydrogen  if we want to make all of that hydrogen  by 2050 green hydrogen then we in the  next 30 years we probably have to  increase the size of our electricity  system by a factor of about four i think  that that really is unrealistic the key  thing is we've got to really focus on  ensuring that blue hydrogen has very  high rates of carbon capture we need to  be up at 95 percent uh carbon capture in  the production process for blue hydrogen  in order for it to be part of our  solution to reaching net zero by 2050.  very briefly mike am i likely to get on  a hydrogen-powered plane anytime soon do  you think  it's the technology for that is a long  way off but it will would require  complete redesign of aeroplanes ammonia  using hydrogen to generate ammonia might  be an easier solution but uh low carbon  flying is sadly quite a long way off at  the moment i won't start booking my  tickets yet  okay we will have to leave it there  sorry to cut you off julia we're out of  time but judah king mike berners-lee  thanks both very much indeed  and that's it from me but tomorrow i'll  be joined by climate justice activist  michaela loach and chairman and founding  director of climate change think tank  e3g tom berg but for now thanks for  watching and see you next time  [Music]
 

